// PENDING: from an OPEN SigmaHQ pull request. Not reviewed or merged by SigmaHQ yet.
// Pull request: #6525 Add detection rules for LiteLLM 1.107.0 cook.pth supply-chain compromise
// Pull request URL: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/pull/6525
// Pull request author: ntwindows2
// Change: new rule
// Title: Suspicious LiteLLM Python Startup Hook File Creation
// Sigma ID: a75418a9-e119-4ab7-8b47-c5c824b584d4
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: Gangadhar
// Date: 2026-10-27 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: persistence, privilege-escalation, T1546.018
// Description: Detects creation of known Python startup-hook filenames associated with the compromised LiteLLM
//   1.107.0 package inside "site-packages". The malicious "cook.pth" file contains an executable
//   "import cook" statement that Python processes during ordinary site initialization, allowing the
//   bundled credential collector to run without the victim ever importing LiteLLM directly.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate packages using sitecustomize.py for environment customization
//   - Unrelated Python packages that happen to contain a file named cook.py
//   - Administrative Python environment customization
// References:
//   - https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/10/23/inside-the-litellm-1-107-0-supply-chain-compromise-credential-theft-through-python-startup-hooks/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/3a37619e6c1b7b3fdf640a86b97c539e893f2f4c/rules/windows/file/file_event/file_event_win_litellm_cook_pth_creation.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceFileEvents
| where FolderPath contains "\\site-packages\\" and (FolderPath endswith "\\cook.pth" or FolderPath endswith "\\cook.py" or FolderPath endswith "\\sitecustomize.py")
